\f0\fs24 \cf0 The Dawn of Everything\
\
10/10\
\
If you liked 
\f1\i Sapiens
\f0\i0  or 
\f1\i Guns, Germs, and Steel
\f0\i0  as much as I did, you need to read this book. Essentially, Graeber and Wendrow unfold a fresh look at newly uncovered anthropological research and archeological evidence. It leads to a profound respect of humans \'93pre-civilization\'94 \'85whatever that means.\
\
The book rips apart the standard evolutionist view of human history first communicated by Enlightenment writers like Montesquieu and Rosseau in France, Turgot and Smith in Scotland. We all know it from heart, especially after reading the two popular books I mentioned above: tiny hunter-gatherer bands, then agriculture happens, then we have large, stratified societies, inequality, wide-spread domination, etc. etc.\
\
Turns out our past is much richer and more expansive than this simple story\'85 who would\'92ve thought! I need not explain in too much detail here; just know that the book is jam-packed with examples of the wide expanse of human political arrangements: with agriculture, without agriculture, cities more democratic than Athens before widespread agriculture, societies with strong holds on power and domination (what we would call a state) but ran by hunter-gatherers\'85\
\
I tend to gravitate towards world-shattering, contrarian takes, I\'92ll admit. But this is one of the most shattering critiques with the best and most extensive citations from contemporary scholars. This isn\'92t just two academics going rogue; this is two writers unearthing something closer to the truth, away from the myths that are spun about our past.\
\
I\'92m going to be thinking about the ideas in this book for a long time. Equipped with this new recognition of humanity\'92s depth and breadth, I am only going to further my understanding and education of society from here.}